3. The brief facts of the present case are that on 20.04.2002 the Food Inspectors Sh. Virendra Singh and Sh P N Khatri under the supervision of Local Health Authority/SDM Sh. V Valte along with staff reached at the premises of M/s Madan Store at shop no. 9, near Bus stop, Timarpur, Delhi-110054 where accused Madan Lal Dawar, Vendor cum Proprietor, of the said firm was found conducting business of various food articles including 'Dal Arhar' which were lying stored/exposed for sale for human consumption, in open plastic bag having no label declaration ;
- that FI disclosed his identity and expressed his intention to purchase a sample of commodity from the vendor which was lying in an open polythene bag bearing no label declaration, to which he agreed ;
CA no. 65/2017 Madan Lal Dawar Vs. Food Inspector (PFA) Pages 2 of 9 - that thereafter as per the prescribed procedure under the PFA Act and Rules after giving notice to the vendor/appellant and offer towards the sale price of sample, to which he agreed and accepted the sale consideration, sample was then lifted ;
- that each sample was separately packed, fastened, marked, sealed and necessary documents including Notice as per Form-VI in the presence of appellant/accused alongwith Panchnama proceedings etc was prepared at the spot ;
- that thereafter one counterpart of sample was sent to Public Analyst (PA) in intact condition and other two counterparts were deposited with SDM/LHA ;
- that vide report dated 01.05.2002, the PA found the sample to be adulterated on the ground that it was coloured with synthetic colouring matter "Tartrazine" ;
- that upon receipt of report, SDM/LHA directed investigation which was carried out by Food Inspector and after completion of investigation sanction under Section 20 of the PFA Act was obtained from the Director PFA.

